From b9afa572b0b29adf7fe76215ad9d92b83934826e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 05:19:21 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] dvbridge: never give the FEL enhancement layer up

Profile 7 FEL is the point of native Dolby Vision output here, so the
enhancement layer is never abandoned for a stream any more.

- On any failure of the enhancement layer (unparsable picture order, a
  full queue, a decoder error, a missing enhancement picture) it is
  resynchronized at the next random-access point common to both layers,
  as after a seek, for as long as the stream plays; the wait for that
  point has no bound.
- The base pictures of that gap, and those of a seek that lands before
  such a point, go out instead of being discarded, so the video never
  stops. dvbridge draws such a FEL picture with the base-layer reshaping
  alone only when its caller counts it (dvbridge_map_color() still
  refuses it by default); the frames with the FEL residual reconstructed
  are counted too. When the enhancement layer ends before the base layer,
  its last pictures are shown instead of cutting the video.
- The statistics log and the Dolby Vision page of the player information
  show, for the last 10 s, the FEL presentations with the residual
  reconstructed out of all FEL presentations, and the layers line shows
  when the enhancement layer is resynchronizing.
- Only missing Dolby Vision metadata (RPU) still sends a stream to the
  ordinary output, after 12 consecutive pictures; it now returns to
  native output once the metadata is back for 48 consecutive pictures.

This replaces the per-stream give-up of the enhancement layer in the
preceding commit.
